---
// Operations handbook. Private working page — noindex, out of the sitemap,
// linked from /admin. Written so a competent hire can run this site without
// David. Keep it updated as part of each cycle, not as a one-off.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const listings = await getCollection('listings').catch(() => []);
const faqs = await getCollection('faqs').catch(() => []);
const communities = await getCollection('communities').catch(() => []);

const sections = [
  { id: 'orientation', label: 'Orientation' },
  { id: 'backend',     label: 'Back end — the CMS' },
  { id: 'frontend',    label: 'Front end — the code' },
  { id: 'seo',         label: 'SEO routine' },
  { id: 'gotchas',     label: 'Known traps' },
  { id: 'runbook',     label: 'When something breaks' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Operations Handbook" description="How alot.land is built, edited and maintained." noindex={true}>
  <div class="bg-bg-dark text-white py-10">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-gold mb-2">Internal · not indexed</p>
      <h1 class="font-display font-black text-3xl sm:text-4xl mb-3">Operations Handbook</h1>
      <p class="text-gray-400 max-w-2xl">Everything needed to run alot.land — what the CMS controls, how the site is built, and how the SEO routine works. Written for whoever is doing the work, whether that is David or a hire.</p>
      <nav class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mt-6">
        {sections.map(s => (
          <a href={`#${s.id}`} class="text-xs font-semibold px-3 py-1.5 rounded-lg bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 transition-colors">{s.label}</a>
        ))}
      </nav>
    </div>
  </div>

  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-10 prose prose-gray max-w-none
                  prose-h2:font-display prose-h2:font-black prose-h2:text-bg-dark prose-h2:mt-12
                  prose-h3:font-display prose-h3:font-bold prose-h3:text-bg-dark
                  prose-code:bg-gray-100 prose-code:px-1 prose-code:rounded prose-code:text-sm">

    <h2 id="orientation">Orientation</h2>
    <p>
      alot.land is a static site. Nothing runs on a server: pages are built into plain HTML and served
      from a CDN. That is why it is fast and why there is very little to break.
    </p>
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Code</strong></td><td><code>github.com/alot-land/alot-land</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Framework</strong></td><td>Astro, with Tailwind for styling</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Editing</strong></td><td>Decap CMS at <code>/admin</code>, logged in with Netlify Identity</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Hosting</strong></td><td>Netlify, rebuilds automatically on every push to <code>main</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Images</strong></td><td>Cloudinary, chosen inside the CMS</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Forms &amp; calendars</strong></td><td>GoHighLevel, embedded by URL</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      <strong>The loop:</strong> someone edits in <code>/admin</code> → Decap commits that change to
      GitHub → Netlify sees the commit and rebuilds → the live site updates. Usually one to two
      minutes. If a change is not showing, it is almost always either the build still running or a
      cached browser — check both before assuming something is broken.
    </p>
    <p>
      One repo drives <strong>four</strong> Netlify sites, separated by base directory: the root is
      alot.land, <code>time-audit/</code>, <code>mission-control/</code> and <code>mfda/</code> are the
      others. <code>mfda</code> builds from its own branch, not <code>main</code>.
    </p>

    <h3>These two pages are password-protected</h3>
    <p>
      This handbook and <a href="/seo/">the SEO dashboard</a> sit behind a password. They are working
      pages, not public ones, and <code>noindex</code> alone only stops search engines — it does not
      stop a person with the URL.
    </p>
    <p>
      The gate is a Netlify edge function (<code>netlify/edge-functions/private-pages.js</code>) covering
      <code>/seo</code> and <code>/handbook</code> only. The username is <strong>alot</strong>; the
      password lives in Netlify as the secret environment variable
      <code>PRIVATE_PAGES_PASSWORD</code>, and David holds it. It is write-only in Netlify — it cannot
      be read back out, so if it is lost it gets replaced rather than recovered.
    </p>
    <p>
      It <strong>fails closed</strong>. If the variable is missing or empty for a deploy context, those
      pages return 503 instead of opening. A blank field is never an exposure — but it does mean a new
      deploy context needs the value adding before these pages work there.
    </p>

    <h2 id="backend">Back end — what the CMS controls</h2>
    <p>Everything below is edited at <code>/admin</code> with no code involved.</p>

    <h3>Listings ({listings.length} live)</h3>
    <p>
      One entry per parcel. Creates the page at <code>/listings/&lt;slug&gt;/</code> automatically, and
      the same entry feeds the homepage, the listings index, the community page and any sales page for
      its community. Edit once, it updates everywhere.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Status</strong> drives the badge, the page title and the social-share stamp. Sold listings are hidden from the homepage automatically.</li>
      <li><strong>Featured on Homepage</strong> is the only control over what appears in the homepage row.</li>
      <li><strong>GPS</strong> as <code>lat,lng</code> places the map pin. Without it, no pin.</li>
      <li><strong>Address</strong> is optional and has its own show/hide toggle, so it can be stored privately.</li>
      <li><strong>Community</strong> links the parcel to a community and switches on the gold banner at the top of the listing.</li>
      <li><strong>Never put the price in the description text.</strong> The price field already appears in four places. A price typed into prose goes stale the moment the field changes, and it looks like the site is wrong.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Sugar Tree Vista sales page</h3>
    <p>
      Settings → Sugar Tree Vista. The whole page is editable: <strong>18 section panels</strong>, each
      with a show/hide toggle and its own copy, plus a drag list that sets the order sections appear in.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Buttons have a text field and a <strong>destination dropdown</strong> — pick a section by name rather than typing a link. Clearing the text removes the button.</li>
      <li>A button pointing at a hidden section automatically redirects to the booking calendar, so it can never dead-end.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;em&gt;word&lt;/em&gt;</code> in a heading gives the gold italic accent.</li>
      <li>Leaving a field blank restores its built-in wording — blanking a field cannot leave the page empty.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>FAQs ({faqs.length} live)</h3>
    <p>
      Categorised <em>buying</em> or <em>selling</em>. They appear on the pages that match their intent —
      selling on <code>/sell-land</code>, buying on <code>/find-land</code> and <code>/listings</code>,
      everything on the homepage — and each page emits its own FAQ markup for search engines.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each FAQ takes a <strong>Video Answer URL</strong>. Paste a YouTube link and the clip plays inside
      the accordion and adds video markup that attributes the answer to a named person. This matters
      more than it looks: it is the difference between anonymous text and a real person answering.
    </p>

    <h3>Communities ({communities.length} live)</h3>
    <p>
      Groups listings and creates <code>/communities/&lt;slug&gt;/</code>. The <strong>Button Link</strong>
      field is what makes the gold banner appear on every listing in that community.
    </p>

    <h3>Images — one rule that matters</h3>
    <p>
      Upload through the CMS so images go to Cloudinary. The media library is configured to cap width at
      1600px automatically. This is not cosmetic: an uncapped phone photo is <strong>2.4 MB</strong>, and
      capped it is <strong>384 KB</strong> — the difference between a lot page loading on rural cell
      signal and being abandoned. If a Cloudinary URL ever appears without
      <code>w_1600,c_limit</code>, it was added by hand and should be fixed.
    </p>

    <h2 id="frontend">Front end — where things live</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>src/pages/</code></td><td>One file per URL. <code>[slug].astro</code> generates a page per content entry.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/content/</code></td><td>The content itself — listings, FAQs, communities, settings. This is what the CMS writes to.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/content/config.ts</code></td><td>Defines what fields exist and their types.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/components/</code></td><td>Reused pieces — header, footer, FAQ accordion, listing card.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/layouts/BaseLayout.astro</code></td><td>Wraps most pages. Owns the site-wide structured data.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>public/admin/config.yml</code></td><td>Defines every field in the CMS.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>seo-engine/</code></td><td>SEO state, metrics history, queue and log.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      <strong>Adding a field is always two edits.</strong> <code>config.ts</code> defines it and
      <code>config.yml</code> exposes it in the CMS. Change one without the other and the field either
      silently disappears when saved, or fails the build. This is the single most common mistake when
      working on this site.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>/sugar-tree-vista</code> is deliberately standalone — it has its own HTML and styles and does
      not use <code>BaseLayout</code>. That means it inherits nothing, including structured data, which
      has to be declared on the page itself.
    </p>

    <h2 id="seo">The SEO routine</h2>
    <p>
      The goal is being <em>quoted</em> by AI search, not only ranked. The method is one deliberate
      improvement a week, published slowly enough to be indistinguishable from a person working.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Holds</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>seo-engine/state.json</code></td><td>Where the programme is: cycle number, target keywords, what has run.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>seo-engine/history.json</code></td><td>Weekly measurements. Written by the Monday job, never by hand.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>seo-engine/queue.md</code></td><td>Everything waiting on a person.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>seo-engine/log.md</code></td><td>What each cycle changed, newest first.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>seo-engine/cycle.md</code></td><td>The runbook the weekly job follows. Edit this to change how the routine behaves.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>seo-engine/review.json</code></td><td>Open pull requests. Written by an Action; drives the dashboard banner.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      <a href="/seo/">The tracking dashboard</a> reads those files and is the fastest way to see where
      things stand.
    </p>

    <h3>How a cycle actually runs</h3>
    <p>
      A scheduled job runs the cycle every <strong>Monday morning</strong>, following
      <code>seo-engine/cycle.md</code> in the repo — the rules live there, not inside the job, so
      changing how the routine behaves is an ordinary edit anyone can review.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>5:00 AM</strong> — GitHub Actions collects the week's metrics and commits them.</li>
      <li><strong>9:00 AM</strong> — the cycle reads that state, picks one move, drafts it, checks the build, and opens <em>one pull request</em>.</li>
      <li>It appears as a gold banner at the top of <a href="/seo/">the dashboard</a>, and GitHub emails you.</li>
      <li>Merging publishes it. Closing discards it. Nothing goes live on its own.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Two refusals are built in on purpose. If a pull request is <strong>already waiting</strong>, the
      next run does not open another — an unreviewed stack is how a queue stops being used. And if the
      build does not pass, it opens nothing: a bad commit here does not show as a broken site, it shows
      as a <em>frozen</em> one, because Netlify keeps serving the last good deploy.
    </p>
    <p>
      The scheduled job runs on David's machine and only while the app is open. If it is closed on
      Monday, the cycle runs at the next launch rather than being skipped. The metrics half runs on
      GitHub and is unaffected.
    </p>

    <h3>Rules that are not negotiable</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>One new page per cycle, maximum.</strong> Publishing velocity is what scaled-content detection keys on. Ten pages in a week gets flagged on the pattern regardless of quality.</li>
      <li><strong>Improve before you add.</strong> If a page already answers the query, make that page better rather than building a competitor to it.</li>
      <li><strong>Publishing nothing is allowed.</strong> Automated SEO gets sites penalised because it feels obliged to produce output weekly. If nothing clears the bar, ship nothing and say so.</li>
      <li><strong>Answer blocks.</strong> Every section and FAQ opens with 40–60 words that make sense read alone. Models quote passages, not pages — a passage that needs its surroundings cannot be extracted.</li>
      <li><strong>Question-shaped headings.</strong> "What does land actually cost?" beats "Pricing".</li>
      <li><strong>Never serve different content to crawlers.</strong> That is cloaking, and it is one of the few things that earns a manual penalty rather than just poor ranking.</li>
      <li><strong>Off-site work is drafted, never posted.</strong> Review requests and profile posts get written and queued for a human.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Words that fail review</h3>
    <p class="text-sm">
      <em>in today's fast-paced world · look no further · elevate your space · nestled in · boasts ·
      unlock · in conclusion · dive into · when it comes to · rest assured · top-notch ·
      state-of-the-art · game-changer · seamless · robust solution · take it to the next level</em>
    </p>

    <h3>Measuring honestly</h3>
    <p>
      Every metrics run also measures a <strong>control domain</strong>. If the control returns zero, the
      measurement is broken rather than the site being invisible — a parsing bug once made every domain
      look like zero, which reads exactly like failure. The dashboard says plainly whether a run is
      trustworthy. Expect flat numbers for the first month; that is normal and it is when people quit.
    </p>

    <h2 id="gotchas">Known traps</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Brave caches hard.</strong> A change that "did not work" is usually cached. Hard-refresh, use a private window, or add <code>?x=1</code> to the URL. Check on a phone before concluding anything is broken.</li>
      <li><strong>Decap is pinned to 3.14.1 on purpose.</strong> It used to auto-update, and a release broke the editor completely. Do not change it back to a floating version without testing.</li>
      <li><strong>A CMS save can fail the build.</strong> If a saved value does not match what <code>config.ts</code> expects, the build fails and Netlify keeps serving the old version — so the site looks frozen rather than broken. Check the Actions tab and the Netlify deploy log.</li>
      <li><strong>New GitHub organisations block third-party apps by default.</strong> That silently breaks CMS saving while builds keep working. Fixed at <code>github.com/settings/applications</code> → Netlify → grant the org.</li>
      <li><strong>Git ref corruption.</strong> Occasionally <code>refs/heads/main</code> is replaced by a stale <code>main.lock</code>. The lock holds the correct commit — rename it back to <code>main</code>. Do not re-initialise the repo.</li>
      <li><strong>Website markup and GoHighLevel are separate.</strong> Changing schema, addresses or copy on the site never affects GHL's phone number, chatbot or SMS registration.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="runbook">When something breaks</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Look here first</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>A change is not appearing</td><td>Is the build finished? Then hard-refresh. Then check on another device.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Everything is stuck on an old version</td><td>A failing build. Netlify deploy log, then the Actions tab.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>/admin</code> will not load</td><td>The CMS config is invalid — usually duplicate names or bad indentation in <code>config.yml</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Saving in <code>/admin</code> fails</td><td>GitHub org app permissions, or Git Gateway needs disabling and re-enabling in Netlify Identity.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A page is blank or a map is missing</td><td>A JavaScript error stops everything after it. Open the browser console.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pages loading slowly</td><td>An image without <code>w_1600,c_limit</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="not-prose mt-10 rounded-2xl bg-bg-dark text-white p-6">
      <h2 class="font-display font-black text-xl mb-2">Keeping this current</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-300 text-sm">
        This page lives at <code class="bg-white/10 px-1 rounded">src/pages/handbook.astro</code>. Update
        it in the same change that alters how something works, not afterwards — a handbook that lags the
        system is worse than none, because it is trusted and wrong.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
